<div class="header-banner">
    <h1>Lesson Planner – Theme # Our New Classmates – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<div class="section-title" style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson Identification Table</div>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td><b>Scenario:</b> Our New Classmates</td>
        <td><b>Theme:</b> Where are you from?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        <td></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td colspan="3">
            <b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Explain a useful skill. Interactive: Talk about preferences and familiar topics in a guided class discussion.
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td colspan="3">
            <b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can explain a useful skill learned at school or in the community (e.g., "I learned how to play the drum in music class." "Liam taught me how to solve math problems faster.").
            Can talk about preferences when discussing school activities and routines (e.g., "I prefer morning classes because I have more energy." "I like math more than science because I enjoy solving problems.").
            Can participate in guided discussions to compare school experiences with classmates (e.g., "In my previous school, we had longer breaks." "My new school has a bigger library than my old one.").
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<div class="section-title" style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</div>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td>
                <b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>
                Engagement, Modeling and Clarification
            </td>
            <td>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and starts a quick "Circle Share" activity. Students sit in a circle and each share one thing they like about their school or a school activity they prefer. For example: "I like recess because I can play with my friends." or "I prefer math class because I enjoy solving problems." The teacher encourages using `like`, `prefer`, and `because` to justify preferences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models questions and answers related to personal and school information. For instance, the teacher asks a student: "Where are you from, [Student's Name]?" and then models the answer "I'm from [Hometown]." The teacher also models "What was your previous school like?" and "It was [adjective] but had a [description]." This activates `question_words` like `where`, `what`, and `verbs` such as `come from`, `used to`. The teacher highlights clear pronunciation of words like `school`, `friends`, `classes`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key terms like "previous school" and "hometown".</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 1: "If I say 'previous school', do I mean the school you are in now, or the school you went to before this one?" (Expected answer: "The school before this one.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 2: "Is your 'hometown' the city where you were born or where you live now?" (Expected answer: "It can be both, or the place you feel is your home.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td>5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>
                <b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>
                Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our New Classmates".
            </td>
            <td>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short dialogue or audio recording between two new classmates, Carlos and Sofia, discussing their previous schools and hometowns. The dialogue will include `Present` and `Past Simple` for habits and descriptions, `comparatives` for comparing schools, and key vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Dialogue Snippet:</b><br>
                Carlos: "Hi Sofia! Where are you from?"<br>
                Sofia: "I'm from Santiago. My previous school was a bit small, but it had a very friendly community. What about you?"<br>
                Carlos: "I come from Chame. My old school used to have a big playground. This new school is bigger than my old one, and the library is more modern."<br>
                Sofia: "That's interesting! I used to walk to school, but now I take the bus."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Responding to questions about classmates and schools (e.g., "Where are you from?" "I'm from Santiago." "What was your previous school like?" "It was small but had a big library.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "Where does Sofia come from?" "What is one difference Carlos mentions about his new school?" (Students provide short oral answers.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b> The teacher reads statements based on the dialogue (e.g., "Sofia's previous school was bigger than her new one. [False]"). Students say "True" or "False."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher pays attention to the pronunciation of `verbs` like `comes`, `uses`, `walks`, `takes` and `plurals` like `schools`, `friends` to subtly introduce the /s/, /z/, /ɪz/ sounds.</p>
            </td>
            <td>7-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>
                <b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>
                Accuracy focus on Speaking.
            </td>
            <td>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Present and Past Simple for habits and descriptions; Comparatives and superlatives; Future tense in affirmative and interrogative sentences (basic questions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> `classmate, school, community, subject, classroom, schedule, recess, cafeteria, playground, library, activity, used to, come from, new, friendly, big, small, interesting, boring, helpful, prefer, like more than, explain`.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Practice the endings of plurals and third person singular verbs with /s/, /z/, and /ɪz/ sounds (e.g., "books" /s/, "friends" /z/, "watches" /ɪz/ - *teacher will provide examples focusing on target verbs/nouns*).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion / Guided Questions):</b>
                        The teacher displays sentence starters or questions on the board/projector. In pairs, students complete the sentences or answer the questions orally, focusing on correct grammar and vocabulary.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"In my previous school, we __________ (used to / has) have art class every Tuesday."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"This school is __________ (big / bigger) than my old one."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What subject do you __________ (like / prefer) more?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher drills pronunciation of "used to," "prefers," and "classes," drawing attention to the final /d/ and /z/ sounds.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice (Substitution Drill with Preference):</b>
                        The teacher provides a sentence frame for expressing preferences, e.g., "I prefer [Subject/Activity 1] to [Subject/Activity 2] because I enjoy [Reason - gerund]." Students work in pairs, taking turns substituting different subjects and activities (e.g., `math`, `science`, `reading`, `playing sports`) and giving a reason.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "I prefer `morning classes` to `afternoon classes` because I have more `energy`."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "I like `English` more than `History` because I enjoy `learning new words`."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, correcting pronunciation of `prefers` /z/ and `classes` /ɪz/.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Skill Sharing):</b>
                        Students work in pairs. Partner A asks, "What's a useful skill you learned at school or in your community?" Partner B answers and explains. Then they switch roles.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "I learned how to `play the drum` in `music class`." "Liam taught me how to `solve math problems faster`."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reminds students to use appropriate `verbs` like `learn`, `explain`, `teach`.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td>15-20 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>
                <b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>
                Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our New Classmates".
            </td>
            <td>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "New Classmate Interview and Share." Students work in new pairs. One student acts as an "interviewer" and the other as a "new classmate." They will use a provided "Interview Card" with guiding questions to have a conversation. After 5-7 minutes, they switch roles. Finally, each student introduces their "new classmate" to another pair or to the whole class, sharing 2-3 interesting facts they learned.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Interview Card Questions:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Where do you come from? What was your previous school like? (Was it `big`, `small`, `noisy`, `quiet`, `friendly`?)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What did you `used to` do after school in your hometown?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What is your favorite `subject` here and why? Do you `prefer` morning or afternoon `classes`?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What is one `useful skill` you `learned` at your `previous school` or in your `community`? Can you `explain` it?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">`Will` you `join` any new `activities` or `projects` this year? Which ones?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Actions:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Distributes Interview Cards and reviews questions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Reminds students to use `Present` and `Past Simple`, `comparatives`, and `future tense` correctly, and to focus on clear pronunciation of third-person singular verbs and plurals.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Circulates to provide support, prompt students to elaborate, and gently correct major errors in a communicative way (e.g., "Can you rephrase that?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Encourages `sociolinguistic competence` by prompting students to use polite language (e.g., "Could you tell me...?", "Thank you for sharing.") and to actively listen to their `classmate`s responses.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Facilitates the sharing phase, ensuring each student gets a chance to present information about their `new classmate` using descriptive `adjectives` and comparing `school experiences`.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td>12-15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>
                <b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>
                Formative assessment strategy.
            </td>
            <td>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on oral production and peer assessment during the sharing phase.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the "New Classmate Interview" and then orally present key information about their partner to another pair or the class. Their ability to respond to questions about their `previous school` and `preferences`, `explain` a `useful skill`, and `compare` aspects of `school life` will be assessed.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear articulation, including the /s/, /z/, /ɪz/ sounds for plurals and third-person verbs (e.g., "He `likes`," "She `teaches`," "His `friends`").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> (e.g., `classmate`, `community`, `subject`, `library`, `used to`, `prefer`, `explain`).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and coherence:</b> Able to `explain` a skill and `compare` school experiences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaborative interaction:</b> Participates actively in discussion, asks and answers questions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar accuracy:</b> Appropriate use of `Present` and `Past Simple`, `comparatives`, and `future tense` questions.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket (Oral or written on a sticky note):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new thing you learned about a `classmate` today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or difficult for you when talking about `previous schools` or `useful skills`?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td>5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>
                <b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b><br>
                Student self-reflection and Teacher reflection.
            </td>
            <td>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b> (Guiding questions for learners)</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I actively `participate` in the `discussions` and `interviews` today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I `explain` a `useful skill` and `compare` my `school` with an `old school` in English?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well during the speaking activities? (e.g., "Students were enthusiastic about sharing personal information and using comparatives.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., "Some students struggled with the future tense questions; I need to provide more structured practice. Also, I need to remind them more explicitly about the `s/z/ɪz` pronunciation in future lessons.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students meet the learning outcomes for speaking? How can I ensure more balanced participation?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson focused on speaking about new classmates and school experiences. In Lesson 4 (Writing Composition), students will use the information and vocabulary practiced today to write a short paragraph describing a new classmate or comparing their current school to their previous one, incorporating target grammar and vocabulary.</p>
            </td>
            <td>3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>